  "slug": "microglial-dependent-2f65",
  "summary": "Recent mouse and culture studies link systemic and autoimmune insults to brain immune cells, astrocytes and neuronal excitability.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how peripheral insults and autoantibodies reach brain immune cells, barrier function and glial signaling. Maternal TPOAb exposure in mice causes placental inflammation, fetal BBB disruption and hippocampal microglial activation. Kidney-derived osteopontin, cleaved by lung thrombin, drives hippocampal CCL2 upregulation and monocyte recruitment with delirium-like dysfunction after acute kidney injury. Neonatal SARS-CoV-2 spike exposure alters SOX2 and TBR1 populations regionally without broad BBB changes. Patient CASPR2 autoantibodies deplete the shed ectodomain, disrupting PMCA2 calcium regulation and network excitability. Newly, astrocyte-secreted Cathepsin D supports hippocampal LTP and memory through extracellular matrix remodeling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
